A||||15||Voyd||fiero weight||||5||
Z||000000||Voyd||02-08-2000||10:04 AM||||hi,<P>i was wondering how much my '85 SE auto would weigh, or how much it weighed in official specs. i looked long and hard in my owners manual and cant find this detail.<P>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||penetrator||02-08-2000||10:10 AM||||About 2590 (curb weight, lb)<P>i think<BR>=)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-08-2000||11:46 AM||||Look at the sticker on the driver side door jamb it will list vehicle gross weight.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||02-08-2000||01:28 PM||||Gross weight is maximum weight fully loaded with people and luggage.<P>The site below list the 85 SE 4 cyl as 2505 and the 6 cyl as 2640. No distinction between auto/manual tranny, w/wo AC, etc. though...<BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/MajorSpecs.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/MajorSpecs.htm</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-08-2000||03:22 PM||||I have a manual that shows it as being pretty close to what DRH quoted.  This weight includes a full tank of fuel, oil, etc, but no occupants or luggage.  I've never looked on my door, but I would imagine the weight quoted there is the GVW which is the max allowable (supposedly) weight of car, fluids, occupants, luggage, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000005||olympic||02-08-2000||03:35 PM||||   The next time your driving late at night, pull into one of those truck weighing scales on the side of the road.  There is usually no one there at night and you can drive right up on the scale and weigh your car with you in it!!  There usually a board about 50 yards ahead of the scale which displays the weight.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||02-08-2000||04:37 PM||||GVW on Fiero is wet weight plus 500LBs as I recall. (2x200LB people 100LBs cargo.)<P>which tranny will change it a few pounds. The trannys are not to big a dif in weight from each other. which motor will change it a good chunk.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||02-08-2000||05:39 PM||||olympic,<BR>Are you saying I can just drive my Fiero into one of those truck weigh stations on the side of the highway, and they/it will tell me what it weighs. Is there a cost?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||02-08-2000||06:05 PM||||56 & Voyd,<P>Let me know your RPO codes and I can come up with a pretty good estimation tomorrow.  Even things like cassette player VS. AM/FM are considered.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000009||Phil||02-08-2000||06:44 PM||||The use of truck scales was covered awhile back. A truck stop usually has certified scales and for about $6 they will weigh your car. Mine went 3100 lbs with my 200lb lard butt,luggage and 1/2 tank fuel.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||02-08-2000||07:55 PM||||When I happened to roll up to a closed Washington State Highway Department scale in the middle of the night in my '87 V6 a/c S/E,got out and peeked into the window of the scalehouse,the display read 2780.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||02-08-2000||10:06 PM||||Be careful when pulling into a Weigh Station (as opposed to a pay scale at a truck stop).  It is illegal to pull in there if you aren't a truck.  Also, those scales are certified by the state, so they tend to get a little pissed if you're on them for no reason.  Just trust me on this one.||||reg||1
Z||000012||olympic||02-09-2000||01:06 AM||||fiero56,<BR>   Around here,(Saskatchewan) there are plenty of weigh scales and some are in the middle of no where.  Most are not manned 24 hours a day, and usually are unmanned at night.  You simply pull onto the weighing pad(all 4 wheels should be on the pad) and there should be a "billboard" about 50 yards in front of you that displays your weight in big numbers.  Some scales may not have this "billboard" and you may have to peek inside the building at the reading.  Since there is no one there, there is no charge of course.   I've never got into trouble doing this but this is Saskatchewan and at 3 am there arn't many people around.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Guinea Pig||02-09-2000||04:33 AM||||Having visited Canada eleven times in the last thirteen years, I've found there are a lot of things that are done there that you wouldn't dare do in the U.S. without fear of some form or governmental intervention.  Not to imply there is to much government in the U.S., that wouldn't be American but,......that's a different forum.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||02-10-2000||10:23 PM||||For those who live in the U.S. mid-west farming country, the grain elevators (Co-ops) have scales too. If they're not busy, most of those good ol' boys will let you drive onto the scales for free, if you ask real nice.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cooter||02-10-2000||11:01 PM||||That guy you buy the firewood from will let you weigh your car too. Just pull onto the scales and get a base line weight, go out into the lot and throw a couple of sticks of wood in the trunk and pull back up to the scales to pay and get your "loaded" weight. It is usually good for a laugh from the guys at the scales, and it only costs a dollar or two.||||reg||1
